# =============================================================================
# Stoky — Environment Configuration
# =============================================================================
# Copy to .env:  cp .env.example .env
# Start services: bun run dev:services   (or: bash scripts/dev-local.sh)

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database (PostgreSQL)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Connects to the Docker container started by docker-compose.dev.yml.
# Port 5432 is exposed to localhost so the host-side app and tools can reach it.
#
# **Development only.** Production does not read this file — see the
# production section at the bottom.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://stoky:stoky_dev_password@localhost:5432/stoky?schema=public"

# For full-Docker mode (docker-compose.yml — app also runs in a container):
# DATABASE_URL="postgresql://stoky:stoky_dev_password@db:5432/stoky?schema=public"

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cache (Valkey / Redis-compatible)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Connects to the Docker container started by docker-compose.dev.yml.
# Port 6379 is exposed to localhost.
VALKEY_URL="redis://localhost:6379"

# For full-Docker mode (docker-compose.yml):
# VALKEY_URL="redis://cache:6379"

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Authentication rate limiting (ISSUES.md CRITICAL-4)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Counted in Valkey, per IP and — for sign-in — per account. The defaults below
# are the ones in src/lib/rate-limit.ts and are what ships when these are unset —
# leave them alone unless you have a reason. Raising them raises the exposure of
# the login form.
#
# The per-account limit is the one that bounds a guess spread across many
# addresses: without it, an attacker with a hundred of them gets a hundred
# budgets against one password. It is higher than the per-IP number because a
# household legitimately signs in from a phone, a tablet and a laptop, and this
# counter cannot tell them apart. A successful sign-in clears the counter it was
# charged to (the account's, or a known browser's own) and gives the address
# back the one attempt it spent.
#
# Note that signing in depends on Valkey: the limiter fails closed, so an
# attempt it cannot count is an attempt it refuses. See SECURITY.md.
# RATE_LIMIT_LOGIN_ATTEMPTS="5"
# RATE_LIMIT_LOGIN_WINDOW_SECONDS="900"
# RATE_LIMIT_LOGIN_ACCOUNT_ATTEMPTS="10"
# RATE_LIMIT_LOGIN_ACCOUNT_WINDOW_SECONDS="900"
#
# A browser that has signed in to an account before is counted on a budget of
# its own instead of the per-account one, so somebody who fills that one by
# guessing does not keep the owner out of their own phone. See SECURITY.md.
# RATE_LIMIT_LOGIN_DEVICE_ATTEMPTS="5"
# RATE_LIMIT_LOGIN_DEVICE_WINDOW_SECONDS="900"
# RATE_LIMIT_REGISTER_ATTEMPTS="3"
# RATE_LIMIT_REGISTER_WINDOW_SECONDS="3600"

# Receipt upload, counted per account rather than per address — the caller is
# already signed in, and an account is the thing that can be held responsible.
# One upload is up to 10MB decoded, re-encoded through Bun.Image, written to disk
# and then handed to Tesseract, which is why it is metered at all.
# RATE_LIMIT_UPLOAD_ATTEMPTS="30"
# RATE_LIMIT_UPLOAD_WINDOW_SECONDS="3600"

# Barcode lookups that reach Open Food Facts, per account. Only a code neither
# the household nor the cache already knows is counted; past the limit the
# person fills the product in by hand. It exists because Open Food Facts
# throttles API clients per address, and every lookup leaves from this
# instance's one address. See SECURITY.md.
# RATE_LIMIT_PRODUCT_LOOKUP_ATTEMPTS="60"
# RATE_LIMIT_PRODUCT_LOOKUP_WINDOW_SECONDS="3600"

# Adding a member to a household, counted per admin account. Each call hashes
# the new password with Argon2id (64 MiB) before anything else, a duplicate
# address included, which is why it is metered at all.
# RATE_LIMIT_ADD_MEMBER_ATTEMPTS="20"
# RATE_LIMIT_ADD_MEMBER_WINDOW_SECONDS="3600"

# Changing your own password, counted per account. It checks the current
# password first, so this is how many guesses at it a stolen session gets.
# RATE_LIMIT_PASSWORD_CHANGE_ATTEMPTS="5"
# RATE_LIMIT_PASSWORD_CHANGE_WINDOW_SECONDS="900"

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Who may register: open | first-user | closed (src/lib/auth/registration.ts)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Unset, it is `open` in development and `first-user` in production. Anything
# else is `closed`, and stops a production server starting.
# STOKY_REGISTRATION="open"

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sign-in (Better Auth, src/lib/auth/instance.ts)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# **Development only**, like DATABASE_URL above. In production this has to be
# the address people actually type — `https://` and the DOMAIN set further down
# — and it is required: Better Auth accepts a sign-in from a browser that
# already holds a cookie only at this origin, marks the session cookie Secure
# only when it is https, and without it the sign-in API answers 503. Left unset
# in development it is http://localhost:3000; DEV_ALLOWED_ORIGIN below adds
# the hosts a phone reaches the dev server at.
NEXTAUTH_URL="http://localhost:3000"
# Signs the session cookie and keys the account tags in the security log.
# Generate a secret:  openssl rand -base64 32
# Unset in development, a random key is used until the server stops. Better
# Auth's own BETTER_AUTH_SECRET(S) and AUTH_SECRET are ignored.
NEXTAUTH_SECRET="your-secret-key-here-change-in-production"

# Optional, dev only: extra origins allowed to reach `bun run dev`,
# comma-separated (e.g. this machine's Tailscale hostname and tailnet IPs,
# for testing from a phone — the camera API needs a secure context, and
# `tailscale serve` is the HTTPS door that avoids a hand-made certificate).
# Each host is also a trusted origin for sign-in, as http://<host>:<PORT or
# 3000> and https://<host>. Never read in production.
# DEV_ALLOWED_ORIGIN=my-machine.my-tailnet.ts.net,100.64.0.1

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Production
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Not configured here. Production — the release bundle, and the source build
# that overrides it (docker-compose.prod.yml) — reads deploy/.env, copied from
# deploy/example.env, which derives DATABASE_URL, VALKEY_URL and NEXTAUTH_URL
# from one password and the domain. None of the values in this file belong in
# it. See docs/INSTALL.md.
